1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:02,922
It is June twelfth, twenty twenty five.

2
00:00:02,922 --> 00:00:09,580
In Ahmedabad, India, two hundred and forty two people board a Boeing seven eighty seven, bound for London.

3
00:00:10,700 --> 00:00:15,473
Thirty two seconds after liftoff, it will crash into a hostel full of medical students.

4
00:00:15,473 --> 00:00:17,701
Two hundred and sixty people will die.

5
00:00:18,833 --> 00:00:22,464
One man, in seat eleven A, will walk out of the wreckage alive.

6
00:00:23,599 --> 00:00:26,737
And investigators will find something almost impossible to explain.

7
00:00:26,737 --> 00:00:34,753
In the last seconds of the flight, the switches that feed fuel to both engines moved from run, to cutoff, one second apart.

8
00:00:35,899 --> 00:00:41,744
More than a year later, nobody outside the investigation knows for certain why. Not us.

9
00:00:41,744 --> 00:00:42,913
Not the airline.

10
00:00:42,913 --> 00:00:49,927
Not, it seems, even the dead captain's own family, who are still fighting to understand what really happened.

11
00:00:51,066 --> 00:00:55,102
This is not a video that tells you who is to blame.

12
00:00:55,102 --> 00:00:57,792
Nobody in an official capacity has said, yet.

13
00:00:57,792 --> 00:01:01,828
This is everything that is actually confirmed, and everything that honestly isn't.

14
00:01:02,966 --> 00:01:04,847
You're watching Black Box Breakdown.

15
00:01:05,966 --> 00:01:07,925
Air India 171.

16
00:01:09,066 --> 00:01:11,182
Chapter one. The flight.

17
00:01:12,299 --> 00:01:17,900
Air India Flight one seventy one was a scheduled service from Ahmedabad to London Gatwick.

18
00:01:17,900 --> 00:01:27,607
The aircraft, registration V T A N B, was a Boeing seven eighty seven dash eight, about twelve years old, powered by two General Electric engines.

19
00:01:28,733 --> 00:01:36,305
In the cockpit: Captain Sumeet Sabharwal, fifty six years old, with over fifteen thousand flight hours, monitoring the systems.

20
00:01:36,305 --> 00:01:42,682
First Officer Clive Kunder, thirty two, with around three thousand four hundred hours, flying the aircraft.

21
00:01:43,799 --> 00:01:47,755
Two hundred and thirty passengers were aboard, alongside twelve crew.

22
00:01:47,755 --> 00:01:52,502
One hundred and sixty nine were Indian nationals. Fifty three were British.

23
00:01:52,502 --> 00:01:54,875
Seven were Portuguese. One was Canadian.

24
00:01:55,999 --> 00:02:02,425
At one thirty eight PM local time, the flight departed Ahmedabad's Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel International Airport.

25
00:02:03,566 --> 00:02:05,995
Chapter two. Thirty two seconds.

26
00:02:07,133 --> 00:02:09,556
The aircraft climbed normally at first.

27
00:02:09,556 --> 00:02:12,384
Airport security cameras captured what happened next.

28
00:02:13,533 --> 00:02:22,331
According to India's Aircraft Accident Investigation Bureau, about three seconds after liftoff, both engine fuel control switches moved from the run position to cutoff.

29
00:02:22,331 --> 00:02:24,531
One second apart from each other.

30
00:02:25,666 --> 00:02:30,730
Cutting fuel to a running jet engine in flight does exactly what you'd expect.

31
00:02:30,730 --> 00:02:35,070
Both engines began losing thrust, almost immediately, seconds after leaving the runway.

32
00:02:36,199 --> 00:02:43,288
A Ram Air Turbine, a small emergency propeller that deploys automatically when an aircraft loses primary power, dropped from the fuselage.

33
00:02:43,288 --> 00:02:49,026
It is exactly the kind of thing that only happens when almost everything else has already failed.

34
00:02:50,166 --> 00:02:53,248
The cockpit voice recorder captured an exchange between the two pilots.

35
00:02:53,248 --> 00:02:58,290
One asks the other why he cut off the fuel. The other says he did not do it.

36
00:02:59,433 --> 00:03:03,508
About ten seconds after the cutoff, the switches were moved back to run.

37
00:03:03,508 --> 00:03:08,524
The engines tried to relight. It was already too late for them to recover in time.

38
00:03:09,666 --> 00:03:18,104
Twenty nine seconds after the fuel was cut, the aircraft came down onto the hostel buildings of B J Medical College, in the Meghaninagar area of Ahmedabad.

39
00:03:19,233 --> 00:03:23,974
Two hundred and forty one of the two hundred and forty two people aboard were killed.

40
00:03:23,974 --> 00:03:27,530
Nineteen more died on the ground, among them students in the hostel.

41
00:03:27,530 --> 00:03:29,604
Two hundred and sixty people, in total.

42
00:03:30,733 --> 00:03:39,327
It remains, to this day, the deadliest crash in Air India's own history, and the worst aviation disaster anywhere in the world in a decade.

43
00:03:40,466 --> 00:03:50,027
It is also believed to be the first fatal crash, and the first total loss, of a Boeing seven eighty seven, anywhere, since the aircraft entered service in twenty eleven.

44
00:03:51,166 --> 00:03:53,282
Chapter three. Seat eleven A.

45
00:03:54,399 --> 00:03:55,530
One passenger survived.

46
00:03:55,530 --> 00:04:01,556
A British citizen named Vishwash Kumar Ramesh, seated at eleven A, next to an emergency exit.

47
00:04:02,699 --> 00:04:11,209
In his own words, to the BBC, he said he regained consciousness, unfastened his seatbelt, and forced himself out through a gap in the wreckage.

48
00:04:11,209 --> 00:04:13,932
"It is a miracle," he said. "Isn't it?"

49
00:04:15,066 --> 00:04:20,329
His younger brother, Ajay, was seated a few rows away on the same flight.

50
00:04:20,329 --> 00:04:21,832
He did not survive.

51
00:04:22,966 --> 00:04:26,646
In later interviews, Ramesh has spoken about ongoing trauma.

52
00:04:26,646 --> 00:04:32,370
"I am the luckiest man alive," he has said. "But also, I lost everything."

53
00:04:33,499 --> 00:04:35,537
Chapter four. The report.

54
00:04:36,666 --> 00:04:45,260
India's Aircraft Accident Investigation Bureau published its preliminary report on July twelfth, twenty twenty five, exactly one month after the crash.

55
00:04:46,399 --> 00:04:51,265
By international rules, a preliminary report is required within thirty days.

56
00:04:51,265 --> 00:04:57,456
It is meant to state facts, from the flight recorders. Not conclusions. Not blame.

57
00:04:57,456 --> 00:04:59,225
Not, yet, a cause.

58
00:05:00,366 --> 00:05:02,888
That is exactly what this one did.

59
00:05:02,888 --> 00:05:09,014
It confirmed the timeline of the fuel switches, and the recorded cockpit exchange, in careful, neutral language.

60
00:05:09,014 --> 00:05:10,815
It did not say why.

61
00:05:11,933 --> 00:05:21,964
As of this recording, the full final report, the one meant to state a probable cause, is not expected until October twenty twenty six, over a year after the crash.

62
00:05:23,099 --> 00:05:29,943
India's Supreme Court has also declined, so far, to order the release of the full cockpit voice recorder transcript.

63
00:05:29,943 --> 00:05:34,985
Almost everything you have heard repeated online is a fragment, filtered through secondhand reporting.

64
00:05:36,133 --> 00:05:38,171
Chapter five. The question.

65
00:05:39,300 --> 00:05:41,849
Here is the honest state of the debate.

66
00:05:41,849 --> 00:05:47,267
Some reporting has read that cockpit exchange as consistent with deliberate action by one of the pilots.

67
00:05:47,267 --> 00:05:50,454
That is not an official finding. It is an interpretation.

68
00:05:51,600 --> 00:06:02,063
A different theory, put forward by the Federation of Indian Pilots, argues an electrical fault could have caused the switches to trip on their own, as a symptom, not a cause.

69
00:06:02,063 --> 00:06:04,426
That, too, is not an official finding.

70
00:06:05,566 --> 00:06:08,701
Both readings exist because the actual answer isn't public yet.

71
00:06:08,701 --> 00:06:11,835
Presenting either one as settled fact would simply be wrong.

72
00:06:12,966 --> 00:06:22,057
Captain Sabharwal's father, ninety one years old, has publicly disputed how investigators have described his son, and has taken his concerns to the Supreme Court.

73
00:06:23,199 --> 00:06:34,850
Two pilots' associations in India have publicly called any narrative of deliberate pilot action "reckless and unfounded," and said the crew deserve, in their words, respect, not unfounded character judgement.

74
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:38,634
There is one more thread worth knowing.

75
00:06:38,634 --> 00:06:48,041
In twenty eighteen, seven years before this crash, America's FAA issued an advisory about some Boeing fuel switches being found with a locking feature disengaged.

76
00:06:48,041 --> 00:06:51,803
It was not mandatory. Air India never inspected for it.

77
00:06:52,933 --> 00:07:00,169
Whether that specific issue applied to this specific aircraft is one of the things the final report is still meant to determine.

78
00:07:01,300 --> 00:07:03,728
Chapter six. The aftermath.

79
00:07:04,866 --> 00:07:09,933
Air India paid interim compensation of about thirty thousand dollars to each affected family.

80
00:07:09,933 --> 00:07:19,704
Tata Group, the airline's owner, separately pledged about one hundred twenty thousand dollars per family, and set up a long-term trust for survivors and the affected community.

81
00:07:20,833 --> 00:07:25,839
No regulator, anywhere in the world, grounded the Boeing seven eighty seven fleet.

82
00:07:25,839 --> 00:07:34,312
India's aviation regulator ordered extra inspections on Air India's own aircraft, and later reviewed, but ultimately cleared, the fuel switch design itself.

83
00:07:35,433 --> 00:07:45,683
In September twenty twenty five, families of four passengers filed a lawsuit in Delaware against Boeing and Honeywell, the maker of the fuel switch, alleging negligence in its design.

84
00:07:45,683 --> 00:07:46,744
It remains ongoing.

85
00:07:47,866 --> 00:07:53,701
One year later, on the anniversary of the crash, families and officials gathered at the medical college.

86
00:07:53,701 --> 00:07:57,819
Two hundred and sixty trees were planted. One for every life lost.

87
00:07:58,966 --> 00:08:02,440
What stays with me about this one is how unfinished it still is.

88
00:08:03,566 --> 00:08:08,512
We are used to air disaster stories with an ending. A verdict. A named cause.

89
00:08:08,512 --> 00:08:13,127
This one doesn't have that yet, and honestly, might not for a while longer.

90
00:08:14,266 --> 00:08:17,974
In the meantime, a father is still fighting for his son's name.

91
00:08:17,974 --> 00:08:20,755
A survivor is still living with what he saw.

92
00:08:20,755 --> 00:08:26,935
And two hundred and fifty nine other families are simply waiting, like the rest of us, for an actual answer.

93
00:08:28,066 --> 00:08:31,487
If a confirmed cause changes this story, we'll cover it.

94
00:08:31,487 --> 00:08:40,723
Until then, wait until you see how an earlier generation of investigators cracked an equally impossible case, in our video on the Boeing seven three seven MAX.

95
00:08:40,723 --> 00:08:42,433
Subscribe to Black Box Breakdown.

